<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Sarcoma</journal-id><journal-title>Sarcoma</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1357-714X</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1369-1643</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">18521270</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2395412</article-id>		<article-id pub-id-type="pii">S1357714X99000134</article-id>		<article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1080/13577149977712</article-id>	<article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Oncogenic Osteomalacia as a Harbinger of Recurrent Osteosarcoma</article-title></title-group><contrib-group>		<contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Lamont</surname><given-names>Elizabeth B.</given-names></name><address><email>elamont@medicine.bsd.uchicago.edu</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib>		<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Cavaghan</surname><given-names>Melissa K.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib>		<contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Brockstein</surname><given-names>Bruce E.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib>	</contrib-group>		<aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>			<addr-line>Section of Hematology-Oncology</addr-line>			<addr-line>Department of Medicine</addr-line>			<addr-line>University of Chicago</addr-line>			<addr-line>5841 S. Maryland Avenue, MC2115</addr-line>			<addr-line>Chicago</addr-line>			<addr-line>IL</addr-line>			<addr-line>60637</addr-line>			<country>USA</country>		</aff>		<aff id="aff2"><label>2</label>			<addr-line>Section of Endocr inology</addr-line>			<addr-line>Department of Medicine</addr-line>			<addr-line>University of Chicago</addr-line>			<addr-line>Chicago</addr-line>			<addr-line>IL</addr-line>			<addr-line>60637</addr-line>			<country>USA</country>		</aff>	<pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>9</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>3</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>95</fpage><lpage>99</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1999 Hindawi Publishing Corporation.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1999</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</copyright-holder><license license-type="open-access" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/"><p>This is an open access article distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</p></license></permissions><abstract>		<p>			<italic>Discussion.</italic> Oncogenic osteomalacia is a rare paraneoplastic syndrome            of skeletal demineralization from renal phosphate loss. Patients with this disorder have the            characteristic clinical, laboratory, and radiographic findings of hyperphosphaturic            osteomalacia. Although the pathophysiology has not yet been clearly delineated, a humoral            factor produced by the tumor is suspected to be the cause.</p>		<p>			<italic>Purpose.</italic> We report the first case of oncogenic osteomalacia that             improved with chemotherapy, discuss this paraneoplastic syndrome, and review the             medical literature regarding its etiology.</p>	</abstract></article-meta></front></article>